# Exercise 03 — Boolean evaluation

> **Goal:** write `eval_formula(formula)` that evaluates a propositional formula
> written in Reverse Polish Notation and returns the result.
>
> **Allowed mathematical functions:** None
> **Required complexity:** O(n) time, N/A space

---

## 0. Before you start — this is the pivot of the module

Exercises 00–02 were about bits. This one starts a completely new block: **logic
and formulas**. It is the most important exercise in the project, not because
it's the hardest, but because of what gets built here.

The subject drops a strong hint (the lightbulb box in Chapter VI):

> *"How about using a (binary) Abstract Syntax Tree structure to parse and model
> your formula? … Doing this now could probably prove useful to your
> understanding for the following exercises!"*

That hint is worth taking seriously. **Exercises 04, 05, 06, 07 and 09 all build
on the structure you choose here.** You can pass *this* exercise with ten lines
and no tree — but Exercises 05 and 06 require you to *rewrite* formulas, and
rewriting a flat string is painful where rewriting a tree is natural. Section 4
explains the choice in detail.

Get a pen. Section 10 has paper work, and the tree drawings in particular are
much easier to understand once you've drawn a few yourself.

---

## 1. Vocabulary

| Word | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **proposition** | A statement that is either true or false. *"It is raining"* is one; *"what time is it?"* is not. |
| **propositional formula** | Propositions combined with logical operators, e.g. *"A and (B or C)"*. |
| **operator** (or **connective**) | A symbol combining propositions: and, or, not, … |
| **operand** | A value an operator works on. In `1 & 0`, the operands are `1` and `0`. |
| **arity** | How many operands an operator takes. `!` is *unary* (1), `&` is *binary* (2). |
| **RPN** | Reverse Polish Notation — operators written *after* their operands. |
| **stack** | A list where you only add and remove at one end. "Last in, first out", like a stack of plates. |
| **AST** | Abstract Syntax Tree — a tree representing the structure of a formula. |
| **node / leaf / root** | A point in a tree / a node with no children / the single node at the top. |
| **subtree** | Any node together with everything hanging below it. |
| **recursion** | A function that calls itself on smaller pieces of the problem. |

The symbols used by the subject:

| Symbol | Maths | Name | Arity |
|---|---|---|---|
| `0` | ⊥ | false ("falsum") | — |
| `1` | ⊤ | true ("verum") | — |
| `!` | ¬ | negation | unary |
| `&` | ∧ | conjunction (and) | binary |
| `\|` | ∨ | disjunction (or) | binary |
| `^` | ⊕ | exclusive disjunction (xor) | binary |
| `>` | ⇒ | material condition (implies) | binary |
| `=` | ⇔ | logical equivalence | binary |

---

## 2. The six operators

Four of these you already met as bitwise operators in Exercise 00 — `&`, `|`,
`^` and negation behave identically here, just on single true/false values
instead of 32 bits at once. The two new ones, `>` and `=`, need real
explanation.

### 2.1 The familiar four

**Negation `!` (¬)** — flips the value:

| A | ¬A |
|---|---|
| ⊥ | ⊤ |
| ⊤ | ⊥ |

**Conjunction `&` (∧)** — true only when **both** are true:

| A | B | A ∧ B |
|---|---|---|
| ⊥ | ⊥ | ⊥ |
| ⊥ | ⊤ | ⊥ |
| ⊤ | ⊥ | ⊥ |
| ⊤ | ⊤ | **⊤** |

**Disjunction `|` (∨)** — true when **at least one** is true. Note this is
*inclusive*: "A or B" is still true when both hold, unlike the everyday English
"tea or coffee".

| A | B | A ∨ B |
|---|---|---|
| ⊥ | ⊥ | ⊥ |
| ⊥ | ⊤ | ⊤ |
| ⊤ | ⊥ | ⊤ |
| ⊤ | ⊤ | ⊤ |

**Exclusive disjunction `^` (⊕)** — true when they **differ**. This is the
"tea or coffee, not both" version:

| A | B | A ⊕ B |
|---|---|---|
| ⊥ | ⊥ | ⊥ |
| ⊥ | ⊤ | ⊤ |
| ⊤ | ⊥ | ⊤ |
| ⊤ | ⊤ | ⊥ |

### 2.2 Material condition `>` (⇒) — the one that confuses everyone

Read `A ⇒ B` as **"if A, then B"**.

| A | B | A ⇒ B |
|---|---|---|
| ⊥ | ⊥ | **⊤** |
| ⊥ | ⊤ | **⊤** |
| ⊤ | ⊥ | ⊥ |
| ⊤ | ⊤ | ⊤ |

The top two rows shock everyone the first time: **when A is false, `A ⇒ B` is
true regardless of B.** A false premise implies anything.

Here's the intuition that makes it click. Think of an implication as a
**promise**:

> *"If it rains, I will take my umbrella."*

When has that promise been **broken**? Only in one situation: it rained (A true)
and I didn't take my umbrella (B false). That is the single ⊥ row in the table.

Now consider the days it didn't rain. I took my umbrella anyway — did I break
the promise? No, I never said anything about sunny days. I left it at home — did
I break it? Also no. **A promise about rainy days is not violated by anything
that happens on a sunny day**, so it still counts as kept. Hence ⊤ in both rows
where A is ⊥.

The key mental shift: `⇒` does **not** mean "A causes B". It only records
whether the promise was broken. That's why logicians call it the *material*
condition — it's about truth values, not about meaning or causation.

**The identity to memorize** (it's in the subject's Interlude 00, and you'll
need it in Exercise 05):

```
A ⇒ B   is the same as   ¬A ∨ B
```

Check it against the table: "either the premise fails, or the conclusion holds."
That's exactly how this file implements it: `(not left) or right`.

**`>` is not commutative.** `1 0 >` is false but `0 1 >` is true. Operand order
matters here, and it's the most common source of bugs in this exercise — see
section 6.2.

### 2.3 Logical equivalence `=` (⇔)

Read `A ⇔ B` as **"A if and only if B"** — true when both sides have the *same*
value:

| A | B | A ⇔ B |
|---|---|---|
| ⊥ | ⊥ | **⊤** |
| ⊥ | ⊤ | ⊥ |
| ⊤ | ⊥ | ⊥ |
| ⊤ | ⊤ | **⊤** |

Two useful observations:

- `⇔` is the exact **opposite of `⊕`**. XOR is "they differ", equivalence is
  "they're the same". So `A ⇔ B` equals `¬(A ⊕ B)`.
- The subject's Interlude 00 gives the rewrite you'll need in Exercise 05:
  `(A ⇔ B) ⇔ ((A ⇒ B) ∧ (B ⇒ A))` — a promise in both directions.

---

## 3. Reverse Polish Notation

### 3.1 The idea

Normally we write operators **between** their operands — `1 & 0`. That's called
*infix*. RPN puts the operator **after** both operands: `1 0 &`.

| infix | RPN |
|---|---|
| `1 ∧ 0` | `10&` |
| `1 ∨ 0` | `10\|` |
| `(1 ∨ 0) ∧ 1` | `10\|1&` |
| `1 ∧ (0 ∨ 1)` | `101\|&` |

Look carefully at those last two. In infix they need **parentheses** to tell
them apart. In RPN they don't — the difference is entirely in the order of the
symbols. That's RPN's superpower:

> **RPN needs no parentheses and no precedence rules.** Every formula has exactly
> one reading.

In infix you must know whether `∧` binds tighter than `∨` to read `A ∨ B ∧ C`.
In RPN the question never arises. This is why it's easy for a computer to parse,
and it's also why your pocket calculator from the 1980s probably used it.

### 3.2 How to read RPN by hand

Scan left to right with a stack:

- **See a value?** Push it.
- **See an operator?** Pop the operands it needs, apply it, push the result.

At the end, exactly one value remains: the answer.

Let's do `1011||=` — the fifth subject example — by hand:

| step | char | action | stack (bottom → top) |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | `1` | push ⊤ | ⊤ |
| 1 | `0` | push ⊥ | ⊤, ⊥ |
| 2 | `1` | push ⊤ | ⊤, ⊥, ⊤ |
| 3 | `1` | push ⊤ | ⊤, ⊥, ⊤, ⊤ |
| 4 | `\|` | pop ⊤ and ⊤ → ⊤ ∨ ⊤ = ⊤ | ⊤, ⊥, ⊤ |
| 5 | `\|` | pop ⊤ and ⊥ → ⊥ ∨ ⊤ = ⊤ | ⊤, ⊤ |
| 6 | `=` | pop ⊤ and ⊤ → ⊤ ⇔ ⊤ = ⊤ | ⊤ |

One value left: **true** ✓ (the subject says `true`)

### 3.3 Why the stack empties correctly

A well-formed formula always ends with exactly one value. If you end with **more
than one**, an operator is missing. If you run out of operands **mid-way**, you
have too many operators. Both cases are malformed input, and this implementation
reports them rather than guessing — see section 7.

---

## 4. Two approaches — and why we build a tree

There are two ways to solve this exercise.

### Approach A — evaluate the string directly

Use the stack from section 3.2, but push **booleans** instead of symbols. When
you hit an operator, pop two booleans, compute, push the answer. Ten lines,
done.

### Approach B — build a tree, then evaluate the tree

Same stack walk, but instead of pushing booleans you push **nodes**. An operator
pops its operand nodes and becomes their *parent*. At the end, one tree remains,
and you evaluate it recursively.

### Which one?

For *this* exercise alone, Approach A is simpler and just as correct. **But it's
a dead end**, and here's why.

| Exercise | What it needs | Works on a string? | Works on a tree? |
|---|---|---|---|
| 03 Evaluate | compute one value | ✔ easy | ✔ easy |
| 04 Truth table | evaluate repeatedly with different variable values | ✔ ok | ✔ ok |
| 05 NNF | **rewrite** the formula (De Morgan, etc.) | ✘ painful | ✔ natural |
| 06 CNF | **rewrite** again (distributivity) | ✘ painful | ✔ natural |
| 07 SAT | evaluate repeatedly | ✔ ok | ✔ ok |
| 09 Set eval | same structure, sets instead of booleans | ✔ ok | ✔ ok |

The problem is Exercises 05 and 06. They don't ask you to *compute* a formula —
they ask you to **transform it into an equivalent one**. Take De Morgan's law:

```
¬(A ∧ B)   becomes   ¬A ∨ ¬B
```

On a **tree**, that's a local surgery: find a `¬` node whose child is a `∧`,
replace it with a `∨` node whose two children are negated. A dozen lines of
recursion.

On a **string**, you'd have to find where the sub-formula being negated starts
and ends — which means counting operands and operators backwards through the
text — then splice pieces together. It's doable, and it's miserable, and you'd
do it twice more for the other rewrite rules.

**So we build the tree here**, exactly as the subject suggests. It costs a few
extra lines now and saves a great deal later.

### 4.1 What the tree looks like

The subject itself draws the tree for `⊤ ∧ (⊥ ∨ ⊤)`, i.e. `101|&`:

```
    ∧
   / \
  ⊤   ∨
     / \
    ⊥   ⊤
```

This implementation prints exactly that:

```bash
$ python3 eval_formula.py -t "101|&"

      ∧
      ├── ⊤
      └── ∨
          ├── ⊥
          └── ⊤
```

Notice how the tree makes the structure obvious: the `∧` at the root is the
*last* operation performed, and the leaves are the raw values. Evaluation flows
**upward** from the leaves.

---

## 5. The algorithm

Two phases: **parse** (string → tree), then **evaluate** (tree → boolean).

### 5.1 Parsing

```
for each character, left to right:
    if it is 0 or 1        -> push a leaf node
    if it is !             -> pop 1 node, push a ! node with it as child
    if it is & | ^ > =     -> pop 2 nodes, push an operator node with both
    otherwise              -> error: invalid character

at the end, exactly one node must remain -> that's the root
```

### 5.2 Evaluating

Recursion does all the work, because each operator only needs the *values* of
its children:

```
evaluate(node):
    if node is 0        -> return False
    if node is 1        -> return True
    if node is !        -> return not evaluate(child)
    otherwise           -> compute both children, then apply the operator
```

The recursion naturally works bottom-up: to evaluate the root you must first
evaluate its children, which first evaluate theirs, down to the leaves where
the values are known.

### 5.3 Worked example: `10|1&`

Parsing:

| step | char | action | stack |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | `1` | push ⊤ | ⊤ |
| 1 | `0` | push ⊥ | ⊤, ⊥ |
| 2 | `\|` | pop 2, push subtree | (⊤ ∨ ⊥) |
| 3 | `1` | push ⊤ | (⊤ ∨ ⊥), ⊤ |
| 4 | `&` | pop 2, push subtree | ((⊤ ∨ ⊥) ∧ ⊤) |

Resulting tree:

```
∧
├── ∨
│   ├── ⊤
│   └── ⊥
└── ⊤
```

Evaluating, bottom-up: `⊤ ∨ ⊥` = ⊤, then `⊤ ∧ ⊤` = **⊤**. ✓

Run `python3 eval_formula.py -t "10|1&"` to see all of this printed.

---

## 6. The code

### 6.1 The `Node` class

```python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, symbol, left=None, right=None):
        self.symbol = symbol
        self.left = left
        self.right = right
```

Three shapes of node:

- **leaf** — symbol is `0` or `1`, no children
- **unary** — symbol is `!`, only `left` used
- **binary** — symbol is one of `&|^>=`, both children used

Keeping unary operators in `left` (rather than `right`) is a convention; what
matters is being consistent, because Exercises 05 and 06 will walk these nodes.

### 6.2 The operand-order trap

This is the single most likely bug in this exercise:

```python
right = stack.pop()    # pops FIRST
left = stack.pop()     # pops SECOND
```

A stack is last-in-first-out. In `A B >`, `B` was pushed *last*, so it comes off
*first*. Popping into `left` first would silently reverse every operand pair.

You wouldn't notice with `&`, `|`, `^` or `=` — **they're all commutative**, so
swapping the operands gives the same answer. But `>` is not:

```
10>   is  ⊤ ⇒ ⊥  =  false
01>   is  ⊥ ⇒ ⊤  =  true
```

If you get this backwards, four of your six operators still look perfect and
only `>` misbehaves. That's a nasty bug to chase, so get it right up front —
and if an evaluator tests exactly one thing, `10>` is a good candidate.

### 6.3 The operators in code

```python
if symbol == "&":  return left and right
if symbol == "|":  return left or right
if symbol == "^":  return left != right          # true when they differ
if symbol == ">":  return (not left) or right    # A => B  is  ¬A ∨ B
if symbol == "=":  return left == right          # true when they're the same
```

Note how `^` and `=` fall out as plain comparisons — `!=` is "they differ" and
`==` is "they're the same", which is precisely what those truth tables say. And
`>` is the `¬A ∨ B` identity from section 2.2, written directly.

Unlike Exercises 00 and 01, **there is no operator restriction here** — the
subject only forbids mathematical library functions, and `and`/`or`/`not` are
language keywords, not maths functions.

---

## 7. Invalid formulas

The subject says the behaviour is undefined for invalid input, but *encourages*
printing an error message. This implementation raises `ValueError` with a
specific diagnosis rather than crashing or returning a wrong answer:

```
''      -> empty formula
'1&'    -> '&' at position 1 needs 2 operands, but only 1 available
'11'    -> formula is incomplete: 2 values left on the stack, expected exactly 1
'1A&'   -> invalid character 'A' at position 1 (allowed: 0 1 ! & | ^ > =)
'&'     -> '&' at position 0 needs 2 operands, but only 0 available
```

Three distinct failure modes, worth being able to name:

1. **Not enough operands** — more operators than values
2. **Too many values left** — an operator is missing
3. **Unknown character** — something outside the allowed alphabet

This costs almost nothing to implement and is the kind of polish evaluators
notice, since it's explicitly invited by the subject.

---

## 8. Complexity

The subject requires **O(n) time**, where `n` is the length of the formula
string. Space is N/A.

- **Parsing:** one pass over the string. Each character causes at most one push
  and a bounded number of pops → **O(n)** ✓
- **Evaluation:** each character creates at most one node, so the tree has O(n)
  nodes, and the recursion visits each exactly once → **O(n)** ✓
- **Total:** O(n) + O(n) = **O(n)** ✓

Note that `n` means the **length of the input string** here, not a numeric
value — a different meaning from Exercises 00 and 01, where `n` was the value of
a number. The meaning of `n` always depends on what the input is.

**A word on the amortized pop count:** it might look like an operator "pops
twice" so the cost isn't linear. But every node pushed is popped at most once
overall, so across the whole parse there are at most `n` pushes and `n` pops.
That's the standard amortized argument, and it's a good thing to have ready if
an evaluator probes.

On space: the tree holds O(n) nodes and the recursion depth is at most the
height of the tree, which is O(n) in the worst case (a fully left-leaning
formula). The subject sets no bound, so this is fine.

---

## 9. How to run it

```bash
cd Ex03

python3 eval_formula.py "10&"        # -> False
python3 eval_formula.py "10|"        # -> True
python3 eval_formula.py "1011||="    # -> True
```

> **Quote your formulas.** `|`, `>`, `&` and `^` are special characters in the
> shell — `|` pipes, `>` redirects to a file, `&` backgrounds the command.
> Without quotes, `python3 eval_formula.py 10|` will hang or create a file
> instead of running. This trips people up during defenses, so mention it before
> handing over the keyboard.

**No arguments** — the demo: the five subject examples, then every operator on
every input combination, then the error-handling examples.

**`-t`** — the full breakdown: parse table, tree drawing, readable infix form,
and result.

```bash
python3 eval_formula.py -t "101|&"
```

### The subject's examples, all verified

| Command | Expected | Got |
|---|---|---|
| `eval_formula("10&")` | false | False ✓ |
| `eval_formula("10\|")` | true | True ✓ |
| `eval_formula("11>")` | true | True ✓ |
| `eval_formula("10=")` | false | False ✓ |
| `eval_formula("1011\|\|=")` | true | True ✓ |

All five match.

### Verification performed

The tree-based evaluator was cross-checked against an independent
stack-of-booleans evaluator on **every valid formula of length 1 to 7** —
19,114 formulas. All agree.

---

## 10. Common mistakes to avoid

| Mistake | What happens | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Popping `left` before `right` | Operands reversed; only `>` shows it | `right = pop()` then `left = pop()` |
| Thinking `⊥ ⇒ ⊤` is false | Wrong results for `>` | False implies anything (section 2.2) |
| Treating `\|` as exclusive or | `11\|` gives false instead of true | `\|` is inclusive; `^` is exclusive |
| Forgetting `!` is unary | Pops two operands and corrupts the stack | `!` pops exactly 1 |
| Not quoting formulas in the shell | Command hangs or writes a file | Use `"..."` |
| Evaluating top-down | Operators need their children's *values* first | Recurse to the leaves, then combine |
| Skipping the tree to save time | Ex05 and Ex06 become far harder | Build it now (section 4) |

---

## 11. Do this on paper

1. **Translate to RPN:** write `(A ∧ B) ∨ C`, `¬(A ∨ B)` and `A ⇒ (B ∧ C)` in
   RPN. Then translate back and check you get the original.

2. **Evaluate by hand** using the stack table format from section 3.2:
   `110&|`, `110>&`, `10^1=`. Check each with the program.

3. **Draw the tree** for `11&10&|` before running `-t` on it. Compare.

4. **Convince yourself about `⇒`:** fill in the truth table for `¬A ∨ B` from
   scratch and confirm it matches the `A ⇒ B` table in section 2.2. This
   identity is the basis of Exercise 05.

5. **Find the difference:** evaluate `10>` and `01>` by hand. Explain in one
   sentence why they differ, and why the same experiment on `&` would show
   nothing.

6. **Break it deliberately:** write three invalid formulas, one for each failure
   mode in section 7. Predict the error message, then check.

---

## 12. Peer-review preparation

- **"Explain RPN."** → Operators come after their operands. No parentheses and
  no precedence rules are needed, because every formula has exactly one reading.
- **"How do you parse it?"** → One left-to-right pass with a stack. Values are
  pushed; operators pop their operands and push the result back.
- **"Why did you build a tree?"** → The subject suggests it, and Exercises 05
  and 06 need to *rewrite* formulas. Rewriting is local surgery on a tree and
  painful text-splicing on a string.
- **"Why is `⊥ ⇒ ⊤` true?"** → An implication is a promise, broken only when the
  premise holds and the conclusion fails. Nothing on a sunny day can break a
  promise about rainy days. Equivalently, `A ⇒ B` is `¬A ∨ B`.
- **"Show me operand order matters."** → `10>` is false, `01>` is true. The
  other operators are commutative so they'd hide the bug.
- **"Justify O(n)."** → One pass to parse, one visit per node to evaluate, and
  the tree has at most one node per character.
- **"What happens with a bad formula?"** → Three named failure modes with
  specific messages (section 7).
- **"Show me it works."** → `python3 eval_formula.py` prints the subject's five
  examples plus every operator's full truth table. Then `-t` on anything they
  choose.

---

## 13. What's next

**Exercise 04 (Truth table)** replaces the constants `0`/`1` with variables
`A`–`Z` and prints the result for every possible combination of their values.
The parser you just wrote needs one small change — accept letters as leaves —
and then you loop over `2ⁿ` assignments.

The `Node` class and `parse` function from this exercise carry forward into
Ex04, Ex05, Ex06, Ex07 and Ex09. Since each folder must stand alone, that code
gets copied rather than imported — but it's the *same* code, so any improvement
you make here is worth propagating forward.